okay  he's only just received his ballot paper  but vastly crispy has already decided  he'll be voting to strike with other  Junior doctors he says things can't go  on as they are with the United  Department have 150 patients waiting to  be seen it is heartbreaking it's very  difficult situation to find ourselves in  there is no privacy there is no dignity  to patients who are either lying on the  floor sat on a chair or lying on a  trolley this is not the way we want to  practice medicine he says strikes are  the last resort we must take industrial  action we want to save our profession if  we want to increase our pay and improve  our working conditions but most  importantly if we want to be able to  save the NHS and care for our patients  from here in West Yorkshire to  Shropshire where Simon day works as a  paramedic staff describe a Health  Service in crisis an average day  will involve  some amount of delay at hospital  um  when you convey a patient and a good day  would be an hour or maybe two hours  a bad day has become when you spend your  entire shift with one patient outside  the hospital  Simon will join a picket line again  tomorrow but as the government  introduced plans for new laws to limit  industrial action unions reacted angrily  to comments about the last ambulance  strike employers could not reach  agreement nationally for minimum safety  levels during recent strikes and health  officials were left guessing at the  likely minimum coverage making  contingency planning almost impossible  and putting everyone's constituents  lives at risk  his words described by one Union as an  attack on ambulance workers  and more strikes seem likely as the  government continues to refuse to talk  about pay for this year  the number of Staff in the NHS has  increased in recent years and yet the  government say that's not translating to  outcomes could you and your colleagues  deliver any more than you do currently  no no we can't whether it's paramedics  nurses or doctors we do not have the  capacity or the strength to continue  working at the stands that we're working  at at the moment and that's why as the  health secretary visited workers at a  111 Center the rift between government  and unions was looking increasingly  difficult to heal Becky Johnson Sky News  Leeds

